2026-11-05T07:48:48.023-08:00 [reviewer] SENT topic="s0036_final" correlation_id="9f08f27e-b918-41dc-a25f-06d64317e24d" status="approved" answer="Typosquatting in package registries occurs when malicious actors create packages with intentionally misspelled names that closely resemble legitimate, popular packages. This deception aims to trick developers into installing harmful packages by mimicking trusted names. Common patterns include \"nmp\" (instead of \"npm\"), \"pytho\" (instead of \"python\"), or \"reactjs\" (instead of \"react\"). These misspellings exploit human error during package searches or installations.\n\nEffective mitigation requires proactive measures: always use registry search features with precise queries, verify package origins through official registry links or trusted channels, and enforce strict naming conventions (e.g., requiring consistent capitalization and avoiding common typos). Additionally, developers should validate package signatures and check for recent updates to avoid inadvertently installing compromised packages. These steps significantly reduce the risk of security incidents caused by typosquatting. (148 words)"
