2026-11-05T08:04:18.985-08:00 [reviewer] SENT topic="s0036_final" correlation_id="61810031-f072-49e9-a43e-aca24f2cc22b" status="approved" answer="To remediate the critical code execution flaw in PyYAML's `full_load` (CVE-2020-14343), follow these actions based on the s0036_rel advisory:  \n\n1. **Upgrade PyYAML to 5.4 or later** (the fix was first released in version 5.4). This resolves the arbitrary code execution risk when untrusted YAML is parsed using `full_load` or `FullLoader`.  \n\n2. **Replace `full_load` with `safe_load`** for all YAML parsing operations. `safe_load` uses a restricted loader that prevents malicious payloads, directly addressing the vulnerability\u2019s root cause.  \n\n3. **Validate input YAML** to further mitigate risks, though upgrading and using `safe_load` are the primary fixes. Do not use `full_load` or `FullLoader` in any context.  \n\n*Note: The advisory (VULN-0036-HW9-04) specifies affected versions as `< 5.4`. Upgrading to 5.4+ is sufficient; 6.0+ is not required for this fix.* (148 words)\n\nSource record: VULN-0036-HW9-04 (s0036_rel id 20077): pyyaml affected < 5.4, severity critical. CVE-2020-14343: Arbitrary code execution when untrusted YAML is parsed with full_load or FullLoader. Fixed in 5.4."
2026-11-05T08:04:18.988-08:00 [reviewer] COMMITTED offset after processing correlation_id="61810031-f072-49e9-a43e-aca24f2cc22b" partition=0 offset=125
